{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-1daa6c97-53dd-44b2-a01f-c775f35f3e89","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-ad9632c913b98dcc","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9005-7q1kuzsz/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9005-E002.md","content":"# Determine required preemption semantics and checkpoint overhead\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves which preemption semantics higher-priority workloads\nactually need: full eviction with requeue, or checkpoint-aware graceful\npreemption. The strategy records conflicting field signals and no benchmark of\ncheckpoint overhead, and the answer determines the scope of the preemption\nimplementation (Kueue preemption configuration alone, versus checkpoint\nintegration with the training stack). The deliverable is a requirements summary\nfrom the field plus a checkpoint-overhead benchmark and a semantics\nrecommendation.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Collect and reconcile the conflicting field/customer signals on required\n  preemption semantics for distributed training workloads.\n- Benchmark checkpoint save and restore overhead for representative multi-node\n  training jobs (time, storage, and job-progress loss), since no such benchmark\n  exists today.\n- Characterize what full eviction with requeue costs users of long-running\n  training jobs (lost progress since last checkpoint or job start).\n- Produce a recommendation: full eviction with requeue vs checkpoint-aware\n  graceful preemption, including what an admin-defined preemption policy must\n  be able to express in either case.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Field requirements are documented: which reference customers need which\n  semantics, with the conflicting signals reconciled or explicitly escalated.\n- A checkpoint-overhead benchmark for representative multi-node training jobs\n  is produced, with measured save/restore times and storage footprint.\n- A written recommendation selects the preemption semantics and states the\n  policy surface admins need, so the preemption implementation can be scoped\n  without further discovery.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-4 (P1, higher-priority workloads preempt lower-priority ones per\n  admin-defined policy): this investigation gates the preemption\n  implementation; its outcome determines whether that work is queue-configuration\n  only or also requires checkpoint integration.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":39391,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b4ce97c1d49649e9","agent_id":"a79a016c227d57657","session_id":"e20e0106-1bbf-436d-84aa-f1899f83fce7","uuid":"b485193c-0d8f-4fdc-9f8e-a86bf22878be","timestamp":"2026-10-22T13:29:06.676Z","subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9005 into epics"}
