{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9e54a46c325694d3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tepic_id: RHAISTRAT-9005-E005\n3\ttitle: Admin-defined preemption policy for higher-priority workloads\n4\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9005\n5\tcomponent: Distributed Workloads\n6\tteam: Distributed Workloads\n7\ttype: Implementation\n8\tpriority: P1\n9\tdependencies:\n10\t- RHAISTRAT-9005-E002\n11\tgated_by: RHAISTRAT-9005-E002\n12\tgate_failure_impact:\n13\t  action: rewrite\n14\t  fallback_approach: If the preemption-semantics investigation selects checkpoint-aware\n15\t    graceful preemption, scope rewrites to include checkpoint save/restore integration\n16\t    with the training stack; if it selects full eviction with requeue, scope narrows\n17\t    to queueing-system preemption configuration only.\n18\tai_signals:\n19\t  change_specificity: 0\n20\t  pattern_precedent: 1\n21\t  adapter_pattern: 0\n22\t  existing_foundation: 1\n23\t  open_questions: -1\n24\t  external_dependency: 1\n25\t  human_process_gates: 0\n26\t  repo_access: 1\n27\t  architecture_claims: 1\n28\timplementation_type: null\n29\tinvestigation_signals: null\n30\tai_implementability: High\n31\tai_implementability_score: 4\n32\tjira_key: null\n33\tbranch: null\n34\t---\n35\t# Admin-defined preemption policy for higher-priority workloads\n36\t\n37\t## Description\n38\t\n39\tThis epic implements workload preemption according to an admin-defined policy:\n40\twhen a higher-priority workload needs resources held by a lower-priority one,\n41\tthe lower-priority workload is preempted per the policy and requeued. The\n42\tpreemption semantics (full eviction with requeue versus checkpoint-aware\n43\tgraceful preemption) are determined by the preemption-semantics investigation\n44\tthis epic is gated on; the scope below covers both possibilities, with the\n45\tcheckpoint integration included only if that outcome is selected.\n46\t\n47\t## Scope\n48\t\n49\t- Configure workload priorities and the admin-defined preemption policy in the\n50\t  queueing system (which priority levels may preempt which, and requeue\n51\t  behavior of preempted workloads).\n52\t- Ensure preempted workloads are requeued and later re-admitted when resources\n53\t  free up, without operator intervention.\n54\t- If checkpoint-aware graceful preemption is the selected semantics: integrate\n55\t  checkpoint save on eviction and restore on re-admission for training jobs,\n56\t  using the checkpoint-overhead benchmark to set sensible defaults.\n57\t- Validate that preemption interacts correctly with per-project GPU quota\n58\t  (preempted resources return to the project's quota pool).\n59\t\n60\t## Acceptance Criteria\n61\t\n62\t- Given an admin-defined preemption policy, when a higher-priority workload\n63\t  requires resources held by a lower-priority workload, then the lower-priority\n64\t  workload is preempted according to the policy.\n65\t- Given a preempted workload, when resources become available, then it is\n66\t  requeued and re-admitted automatically.\n67\t- Given preemption occurs, when quota is recomputed, then the preempted\n68\t  workload's GPU allocation is released back to its project's quota.\n69\t- Given checkpoint-aware semantics are selected, when a workload is gracefully\n70\t  preempted, then it resumes from its last checkpoint rather than restarting\n71\t  (or restarts, if full-eviction semantics are selected — per the gating\n72\t  investigation's outcome).\n73\t- Preemption is configurable by admins (policy on/off, which priorities\n74\t  preempt which) via supported configuration.\n75\t\n76\t## HLR Traceability\n77\t\n78\t- HLR-4 (P1, higher-priority workloads can preempt lower-priority ones\n79\t  according to an admin-defined policy): fully covered by this epic.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9850fdc7d99bd18f","agent_id":"a3984109486a77230","session_id":"e20e0106-1bbf-436d-84aa-f1899f83fce7","uuid":"bf45810c-ea3c-4555-b