{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-819a3656be74f052","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9005\n3\tepic_count: 7\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9005 (Queue-aware GPU scheduling for distributed training workloads)\n11\t\n12\tStrategy status: Refinement Done, priority Critical. Not triaged as below-threshold\n13\t(S-sized check fails: scope spans two work streams — scheduling backend across\n14\tDistributed Workloads/Training Operator plus a Dashboard UI integration — and the\n15\tstrategy carries three genuine open questions, one of which explicitly \"changes the\n16\tshape of most implementation work\"). Not docs-only. Full decomposition follows.\n17\t\n18\t## Source Parsing Notes\n19\t\n20\t- The strategy provides: Problem Statement, High-Level Requirements (HLR-1..5),\n21\t  Affected Components (bullet list: Distributed Workloads, Training Operator,\n22\t  Dashboard), Out of Scope, and three Open Questions.\n23\t- **Missing sections (health warning):** no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n24\t  table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements,\n25\t  no Technical Approach, no Staff Engineer Input. Epic acceptance criteria below\n26\t  are derived from the HLRs and problem statement; team assignments are taken\n27\t  from architecture context (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`).\n28\t- All three affected components exist in the platform (architecture context files\n29\t  present), so no onboarding chain (repo-onboarding / license-validation /\n30\t  konflux-onboarding) is generated. All are Konflux-onboarded per architecture\n31\t  context.\n32\t\n33\t## Component Mapping\n34\t\n35\t| Strategy component | Canonical Jira component | Owner team (from architecture context) | Classification |\n36\t|---|---|---|---|\n37\t| Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | Distributed Workloads | Active (Kueue queueing, quota, preemption, placement) |\n38\t| Training Operator | Training Operator | Distributed Workloads | Passive for the Kueue-native path (no code change; jobs must keep working — validation ACs on the gang-admission epics). May become active if the scheduler-plugin branch is selected; that work is inside the branch scope. |\n39\t| Dashboard | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | Active (HLR-3 visibility UI) |\n40\t\n41\tAll epic `component` values are verbatim canonical names from\n42\t`.context/rhai-components.txt`. The scheduler-plugin integration epic\n43\t(BRANCH-B-E009) spans Training Operator (PyTorchJob) and KubeRay (RayJob)\n44\tsurfaces; it is assigned `Distributed Workloads` as the single most-relevant\n45\tcanonical name for the scheduling stack, with the span noted here.\n46\t\n47\t## Epic List\n48\t\n49\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n50\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n51\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E001 | Validate Kueue all-or-nothing admission for multi-node training jobs | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n52\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E002 | Determine required preemption semantics and checkpoint overhead | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n53\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E003 | Assess topology-aware scheduling Tech Preview maturity | Investigation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n54\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E004 | Enforce per-project GPU quota at admission time | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n55\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E005 | Admin-defined preemption policy for higher-priority workloads | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P1 |\n56\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E006 | Topology-aware GPU placement preference | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P2 |\n57\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-E007 | Dashboard visibility into queue position, quota, and admission state | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n58\t\n59\tConditional branch epics (attached to E001 as plan document, not standalone issues):\n60\t\n61\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n62\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n63\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-A-E008 | Kueue-native gang admission configuration for multi-node training jobs | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n64\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E008 | Provision coscheduling scheduler-plugin for gang scheduling | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n65\t| RHAISTRAT-9005-BRANCH-B-E009 | Gang admission integration for training jobs via scheduler plugin | Implementation | Distributed Workloads | P0 |\n66\t\n67\t## Dependency DAG (main plan)\n68\t\n69\t```mermaid\n70\tgraph TD\n71\t    E001[\"E001 Validate gang admission semantics (Investigation)\"]\n72\t    E002[\"E002 Preemption semantics investigation\"]\n73\t    E003[\"E003 Topology TP maturity investigation\"]\n74\t    E004[\"E004 Per-project GPU quota enforcement\"]\n75\t    E005[\"E005 Preemption policy implementation\"]\n76\t    E006[\"E006 Topology-aware placement\"]\n77\t    E007[\"E007 Dashboard queue/quota/admission visibility\"]\n78\t    E002 --> E005\n79\t    E003 --> E006\n80\t    E004 --> E007\n81\t```\n82\t\n83\tBranch DAG (conditional, gated by E001; excluded from the main-plan critical path):\n84\t\n85\t```mermaid\n86\tgraph TD\n87\t    E001[\"E001 Validate gang admission semantics\"]\n88\t    BA[\"BRANCH-A-E008 Kueue-native gang admission (outcome A)\"]\n89\t    BB1[\"BRANCH-B-E008 Provision coscheduling plugin (outcome B)\"]\n90\t    BB2[\"BRANCH-B-E009 Job integration via plugin (outcome B)\"]\n91\t    E001 --> BA\n92\t    E001 --> BB1\n93\t    E001 --> BB2\n94\t    BB1 --> BB2\n95\t```\n96\t\n97\t## DAG Justification\n98\t\n99\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n100\t|---|---|---|\n101\t| E001 → BRANCH-A-E008 | Rule 3 | Gang-admission validation selects the implementation approach; true gate (`rewrite`). Bounded outcomes (Kueue-native sufficient vs scheduler-plugin needed) → conditional branches. |\n102\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E008 | Rules 3, 22 | If Kueue is insufficient, the coscheduling scheduler-plugin is new infrastructure not in the platform inventory → provisioning epic gated on the validation outcome. |\n103\t| E001 → BRANCH-B-E009 | Rule 3 | Job-type integration only exists in the plugin outcome; gated on the same validation. |\n104\t| BRANCH-B-E008 → BRANCH-B-E009 | Rule 12 | Plugin provisioning produces the framework the job integration builds against; sibling edge within branch B. |\n105\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 | Preemption semantics (full eviction+requeue vs checkpoint-aware) rewrites the preemption epic's scope; true gate (`rewrite`). |\n106\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 3 | If Tech Preview topology discovery is not mature enough this release, the placement epic does not ship; true gate (`remove`). |\n107\t| E004 → E007 | Rule 13 | The Dashboard renders the per-project quota model (quota consumption per project across job types); it builds against the quota structures E004 defines — a build-against artifact, not a configuration reference. |\n108\t| No edge E001 → E004 | Rule 14/15 | Per-project quota is enforced by Kueue's ClusterQueue/LocalQueue quota model at admission under either gang-admission outcome; the investigation does not change what E004 builds. Over-serializing would block P0 quota work unnecessarily. |\n109\t| No edges among E004, E005, E006 | Rules 1, 15 | Same component and team but distinct logical changes (quota vs preemption vs placement), different areas of the queueing configuration; parallel. |\n110\t| No edge E001 → E007 | — | The Dashboard reads Kueue Workload CR state and queue metrics, which expose pending/admitted state under either admission mechanism; the outcome does not change the Dashboard surface. |\n111\t| No konflux-onboarding chain | Rule 10 | All components are already in the platform and Konflux-onboarded; no pipeline onboarding epics exist, so no build-pipeline coupling edges. |\n112\t\n113\tCross-feature validation ordering (revision note): the preemption epic's\n114\tquota-release criterion and the branch-B job-integration epic's\n115\tquota-preservation criterion both verify the per-project GPU quota\n116\tenforcement deliverable's output. Neither epic's construction consumes that\n117\toutput, so no DAG edges were added; each epic body now carries an explicit\n118\tsequencing note that the quota-interaction criterion is validated only after\n119\tthe per-project GPU quota enforcement work has landed, so failures localize\n120\tcorrectly.\n121\t\n122\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n123\t\n124\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n125\t|---|---|---|\n126\t| HLR-1 (all-or-nothing admission) | P0 | BRANCH-A-E008 (outcome A) or BRANCH-B-E008 + BRANCH-B-E009 (outcome B) — selected by E001 |\n127\t| HLR-2 (per-project GPU quota at admission) | P0 | E004 |\n128\t| HLR-3 (Dashboard queue/quota/admission visibility) | P1 | E007 |\n129\t| HLR-4 (admin-defined preemption) | P1 | E005 (gated by E002) |\n130\t| HLR-5 (topology-aware placement) | P2 | E006 (gated by E003) |\n131\t\n132\tAll five HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-1 is covered by exactly\n133\tone branch per outcome — the branch epics are mutually exclusive alternatives,\n134\tnot additive scope.\n135\t\n136\t## Priority Inheritance\n137\t\n138\t- E001 is P0: it gates all HLR-1 (P0) work via the branch epics.\n139\t- E002 is P1: it gates only HLR-4 (P1).\n140\t- E003 is P2: it gates only HLR-5 (P2).\n141\t- E004 is P0 on its own HLR-2 merit (it also enables E007/HLR-3 at P1 — the\n142\t  higher P0 wins).\n143\t- Branch epics are P0 (HLR-1); they are conditional alternatives and excluded\n144\t  from the main-plan critical path.\n145\t\n146\t## Health Warnings\n147\t\n148\t1. **Missing strategy sections** (non-blocking): no Acceptance Criteria, NFRs,\n149\t   Impacted Teams, or Dependencies table in the source strategy — epic ACs are\n150\t   derived from HLRs and the problem statement; teams from architecture context.\n151\t2. **\"Target scale\" undefined**: the